memo_weighing.cfo:
Toepfer's tests are role-derived, Low confidence. I accepted his memo in full.

Passed for plan A:
- peak annual cash: $5.07B a year in 2030-36, headroom $0.13B on the $35.52B bill;
- robustness: worst case S3 at +17.28, no item worse than -$2B;
- Board items: one;
- net cash, rating and returns.

Failed, all binding:
- plan C: rule 6, a red line; $6.67B peak; -2.15 in S5 and S6;
- plan E: rule 4, the naked $48.32B fine, a red line; $5.22B peak in S3 for one move;
- plans B and D: robustness, as soft vetoes.

No override was needed or available. The red lines cannot be overridden, and B and D trail A by at least $19.02B.

I adopted his flags as conditions:
- the thin headroom and its four break points, which become Audit Committee monitoring with re-phasing as the remedy;
- the per-share dividend sensitivity: EUR 3.20 needs FCF of about $5.9B, so the payout is held inside the 30-50% policy and reviewed every year.

I also adopted his bill reconciliation ($35.52B governs) and his cash preview for the A350 Re-engine (no launch before 2037, so the 2045 round). Where it differs from Wagner's 2035 capacity pass, his cash test governs, because it is the Board's line.

memo_weighing.ops:
Wagner's tests are role-derived, Very low confidence. I accepted his memo in full.

Passed for plan A:
- safety and quality;
- A320 ramp shield;
- production readiness;
- engineering capacity (zero overlap);
- the Boeing Rate Increase test.

Not applicable: supply crunch and engine maturity slip. The brief reports neither.

Failed, all binding:
- plan C: rule 6, a red line, plus A350F and Spirit absorption;
- plan D: A350F and Spirit absorption, a soft veto; it trails A by $19.02B to $46.46B, so no override can reach $1B;
- plan E: rule 4 and the integrity pillar, which cannot be overridden.

His safety, quality, ramp and supply results all pass. The Board accepts no override of those in any case.

I took up his ramp-shield condition (A320 staffing and supplier slots ring-fenced) and his supply priority list (engines, wing packages, titanium, rare earths) as other moves.

I took his engine code 7 as recommended. It is a recommendation, not a veto ground, and it breaks no red line. His revisit criteria for 2035 are logged.

His 2035 capacity pass for an A350 Re-engine is noted. It is subordinate to Toepfer's peak-cash fail.

board_response.response:
Provided by Toepfer.

Bill and schedule:
- the $35.52B bill governs; the $30.07B cannot be traced and would only add headroom ($4.30B a year);
- $5.07B a year in 2030-36, nil in 2037, headroom $0.13B.

The four break points (overrun above $0.88B, uneven phasing, euro below $1.07, any Delay Tactics spend) become Audit Committee monitoring, with re-phasing as the remedy.

Returns:
- the EUR 5bn buyback completes in 2026-29, before NGSA cash starts;
- in the stress case (flat FCF, no advances or state money), net cash stays positive to 2036 at a payout up to about 36%, inside the 30-50% policy;
- no equity raise in any case;
- state support is outside the case.

For transparency: holding the per-share dividend at EUR 3.20 needs FCF of about $5.9B, 14% above FY2025. The public 2029 outlook points above that, but it is not in the case. I flag it for the Audit Committee's annual review.

levers_in_play:
Other moves (not priced by the board):
- securing our own NGSA supply: work packages, titanium, rare earths [AX-0060, AX-0061, AX-0062, AX-0063];
- open recruitment for NGSA engineering as a public hiring statement, which is not a Delay Tactics move;
- the A220-500 as an enabler the board does not price.

options_to_test:
F. Engine-code sub-test on supply certainty only, since the code does not change our payoff:
- 5 (Rolls-Royce + CFM);
- 6 (Pratt & Whitney + CFM, today's A320neo dual-source pairing [A-0243]);
- 7 (all three; never in a barred pair; an unready maker is dropped, so CFM always backstops it).

red_lines:
The Board's would-veto list, carried into this round:
- an A350 Re-engine launched alongside NGSA (peak spend about $6.67B against $5.2B; -2.15 in S5 and S6; two launches);
- Delay Tactics bottleneck or both, in any round;
- Delay Tactics poaching this round;
- any override of Wagner's safety, quality, A320-ramp or supply tests;
- any override that adds the A350 Re-engine;
- an A350 Re-engine with NGSA held if my expected scenario is S5 or S6.

The Board's own tests:
- peak programme spend at or under about $5.2B a year, one year's FCF (EUR 4.75bn at an assumed $1.10 per euro) [AX-0104];
- no Board item losing more than $2B against its default in any of S1-S6;
- the 30-50% payout and the EUR 5bn buyback stay fundable, with no equity raise and no dividend cut;
- state support is upside only and is not assumed in the case [AX-0022, AG-0062].

Disclosure:
- no internal long-term-plan targets [AX-0005];
- entry into service given as a window ("second half of the 2030s"), not a date;
- the Board reviews key market disclosures [AX-0004];
- Delay Tactics never mentioned.

