boeing.premium_reason:
This is a doctrine premium of $0.46B in the expected column S4, and the same in S2: launch_10yr (-1.96) against launch_7yr (-1.50), measured against the best plan that respects the hard rules and the Board's tests.

Grounds:
- doing less and doing it better [BX-1239]: a bill $4.68B smaller and a debt penalty $2.9B lower undiscounted;
- right than fast, with headroom [BX-1241, BX-1275]: about $6.0B a year of spend against $9.3B;
- balance sheet first [BX-1305, BX-0556];
- a 2040 date that holds under doctrine's clean-sheet slip prior [BX-0553].

The premium is nil in S1, S3 and S5, where the 10-year is ahead. There is no objective premium, since no plan reaches 50% by 2040.

Logged separately as a hard-rule cost, not a premium: the 787 Re-engine forgone under my H3 ruling, 2.20 in S1, S2 and S5 and nil in S3 and S4. The total doctrine plus objective premium is 0.46, within the $2B cap (H8). The figure holds only if the fps schedule does not slip, which the board does not price.

board_response.response:
Taken up in full.

H1 is lifted for 2030:
- 777X first delivery in 2027 [BX-0550];
- MAX 7/10 anti-ice certified in 2026 [BX-1873, BX-1299];
- 777X cash near neutral in 2028 and positive from 2029 [BX-0547];
- debt repair on the path I set (about two years, launch around 2029-30) [BG-0068, B-2327, BX-1305];
- market, technology and finances all ready [B-2303, BX-1296].

H3's first half is held for Round 1 on a re-founded ground: one development at a time, and from 2030 the program in hand is the fps [B-0341, B-1526, B-2326, BX-1239]. H3's second half applies as written.

frame.situation:
Decision year 2030, Round 1. Nobody has launched anything. Observed shares were flat over 2026-29: narrowbody 40/60, widebody 58.8/41.2, engines unchanged. The brief reports no live quality, FAA, supply-chain or certification problem. The game board has no FAA, so my FAA-process gate passes [BX-1309].

Do Nothing scores 0.00 only if the rivals hold (S1). It scores -3.64 if Airbus launches NGSA (S2), -2.62 against an A350 Re-engine (S3), -6.26 against both (S4) and -3.64 in the risk case, NGSA plus Delay Tactics (S5). Waiting is not free.

Grid against Do Nothing, by column (S1 / S2 / S3 / S4 / S5):
- fps launch_10yr alone: +3.50 / +4.30 / +3.50 / +4.30 / +2.86.
- fps launch_7yr alone: +2.20 / +4.76 / +2.20 / +4.76 / +2.81.
- Both Solo ramps beat Do Nothing by more than epsilon in every column, and both clear the S5 floor of -5.64 (10-year -0.78, 7-year -0.83).
- fps via Embraer: -14.55 / -13.75 / -14.56 / -13.75 / -15.19, and -18.83 in S5. It fails the buffer test outright.
- 737 Rate Increase: -0.74 in every column when fps is launched. With fps on hold it is +2.30 in S1 and S3 and -0.74 in S2, S4 and S5.
- 787 Re-engine on top of the 10-year fps: +2.20 in S1, S2 and S5, but -3.58 in S3 and S4. The strain overlap is 2 years on the board's measure. On top of the 7-year fps it is +1.27 / -4.51 with a 5-year overlap.
- Debt penalty and two-front strain are already netted in the grid. I will not subtract them again. For scale only, the undiscounted penalties in dashboard_game.md §2 are: 10-year $19.1B, 7-year $22.0B, via Embraer $52.5B. Debt is $45B on an EV of $130B.

Objective (the company's mission, not my ranking). Holding 50/50 is not met at 40%; incumbency is met. No plan can reach 50% by 2040: the earliest fps entry into service is 2037, and capture is 1 pp a year.

The record I bring into 2030:
- 777X first delivery in 2027 [BX-0550];
- MAX 7/10 anti-ice certification in 2026 [BX-1873, BX-1299];
- 777X cash near neutral in 2028 and positive from 2029 [BX-0547];
- my own July 2026 view: about two more years to financial readiness, with launch around 2029-30 [BG-0068];
- Spirit closed December 2025 [BG-0064];
- 47 a month cleared in May 2026 [BG-0069].

Board risks (from its guidance; they are not my red lines). It would veto:
- launch_via_embraer in any combination;
- re787: launch in any combination this round (Downside test fails in S3 and S4);
- rate_737: increase paired with any fps launch, or with fps on hold when the expected column is S2, S4 or S5;
- any launch without my written H1 ruling or without Pope's readiness results;
- any premium above $2B;
- any plan with a binding ExCo veto still standing.
It would approve one Solo fps on either ramp, standing alone.

red_lines:
My own lines:
- no rate request without stable KPIs [BX-1259, BX-1271];
- no production-certification concurrency in the fps plan [BX-1263];
- no new airplane before debt repair and finished developments (folded into the H1 ruling) [BX-1247];
- no fixed-price development [BX-1262, BX-1304];
- no blame on the FAA [BX-1323];
- no attribution of a supplier problem to Airbus before exposure (doctrine);
- no order justified by Airbus's timing [B-2313, B-2315].

